{
 "nodes": [
  {
   "nodeID": "290",
   "text": "Why does China's surplus look small in IMF imbalance data when trade flows show it is huge?",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "question",
   "toulmin": "framing",
   "oper": "framing",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#Q"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "291",
   "text": "A large real estate crisis typically pushes a country to rely more on exports (standard economic theory)",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "stated_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#P1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "292",
   "text": "Customs goods data captures China's real imbalance better than balance of payments data",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "stated_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#P2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "293",
   "text": "Opaque official statistics that no one can replicate deserve less trust than physical trade flows",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "hidden_assumption",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.prior",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#P3"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "294",
   "text": "China's 2024 current account surplus about $430 billion (just over 2 percent of GDP) versus the EU's $500 billion; goods and services surplus about $650 billion versus the EU's $620 billion; about $4 trillion in net interest-paying foreign assets, yet an investment income deficit of $150 to 200 billion; Chinese customs data show about $400 per phone; net vehicle exports above 6 million",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "evidence_data",
   "toulmin": "grounds",
   "oper": "grounds",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#E1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "295",
   "text": "IMF 2024 Article IV report; China's State Administration of Foreign Exchange argument about Apple; European Central Bank (Philip Lane) analysis of Ireland; Kenneth Rogoff in the IMF's Finance and Development",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "evidence_source",
   "toulmin": "grounds",
   "oper": "grounds",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#E2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "296",
   "text": "An unexplained 2021 break in China's balance of payments goods series and an unexplained investment income deficit shrink China's reported current account",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "mechanism",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.mechanism",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#M1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "297",
   "text": "U.S. multinationals in Ireland (Apple, pharmaceutical companies) inflate the EU's goods surplus and hide its investment income surplus",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "mechanism",
   "toulmin": "warrant",
   "oper": "warrant.mechanism",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#M2"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "298",
   "text": "Current account comparisons based on balance of payments data make Europe, not China, look like the main source of imbalance",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "intermediate_claim",
   "toulmin": "claim",
   "oper": "claim.step",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#C1"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "299",
   "text": "The real imbalance is in China; the IMF and G7 should focus there",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "main_claim",
   "toulmin": "claim",
   "oper": "claim.final",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#K"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "300",
   "text": "The IMF and analysts using comprehensive goods and services data, who see the EU as the biggest contributor",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "opposing_view",
   "toulmin": "rebuttal",
   "oper": "rebuttal",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#X"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "301",
   "text": "Balance of payments adjustments may or may not be more accurate, and some are legitimate (insurance and freight, Airbus)",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "caveat",
   "toulmin": "qualifier",
   "oper": "qualifier",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#QF"
  },
  {
   "nodeID": "302",
   "text": "China's services deficit, mostly travel, genuinely reduces its surplus",
   "type": "I",
   "role": "other_explanation",
   "toulmin": "rebuttal",
   "oper": "rebuttal",
   "localID": "setser_2025-07-07_china-stealth-surplus#R1"
  },
